WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.176
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online IELTS preparation.

2
00:00:05.476 --> 00:00:09.037
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.337 --> 00:00:17.028
Unit seven: Reading: True, False and Not Given. Today you will decide whether the text agrees, disagrees or says nothing.

4
00:00:17.328 --> 00:00:19.910
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.867 --> 00:00:24.471
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.317 --> 00:00:26.376
Statement.

7
00:00:27.383 --> 00:00:30.486
Statement means a sentence you must check against the text.

8
00:00:31.083 --> 00:00:32.874
Read the statement carefully.

9
00:00:33.483 --> 00:00:35.578
Your turn. Say: Statement.

10
00:00:38.433 --> 00:00:39.504
Statement.

11
00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:40.798
True.

12
00:00:41.783 --> 00:00:44.154
True means the text agrees with the statement.

13
00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.204
The answer is True.

14
00:00:46.817 --> 00:00:49.330
Your turn. Say: True.

15
00:00:52.167 --> 00:00:52.796
True.

16
00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:54.447
False.

17
00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:57.902
False means the text says the opposite.

18
00:00:58.517 --> 00:00:59.982
The answer is False.

19
00:01:00.583 --> 00:01:02.783
Your turn. Say: False.

20
00:01:05.633 --> 00:01:06.611
False.

21
00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:08.351
Not Given.

22
00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:12.557
Not Given means the text has no information about it.

23
00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:15.428
If it is not mentioned, write Not Given.

24
00:01:16.017 --> 00:01:19.064
Your turn. Say: Not Given.

25
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.064
Not Given.

26
00:01:23.817 --> 00:01:24.864
Contradict.

27
00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.395
Contradict means to say the opposite of something.

28
00:01:28.983 --> 00:01:31.087
The text contradicts the statement.

29
00:01:31.683 --> 00:01:34.103
Your turn. Say: Contradict.

30
00:01:36.967 --> 00:01:38.037
Contradict.

31
00:01:38.783 --> 00:01:39.506
Assume.

32
00:01:40.517 --> 00:01:42.667
Assume means to believe without proof.

33
00:01:43.283 --> 00:01:44.726
Do not assume anything.

34
00:01:45.317 --> 00:01:48.143
Your turn. Say: Assume.

35
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:51.804
Assume.

36
00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:53.435
Evidence.

37
00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:57.042
Evidence means facts that support an answer.

38
00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.452
Find evidence in the text.

39
00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:02.424
Your turn. Say: Evidence.

40
00:02:05.267 --> 00:02:06.210
Evidence.

41
00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:08.183
Exaggerate.

42
00:02:09.183 --> 00:02:12.831
Exaggerate means to make something seem bigger or more extreme.

43
00:02:13.417 --> 00:02:15.765
The statement exaggerates the facts.

44
00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:19.176
Your turn. Say: Exaggerate.

45
00:02:22.033 --> 00:02:23.139
Exaggerate.

46
00:02:23.933 --> 00:02:27.886
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:32.224
Visal, what is the hardest part of True, False, Not Given?

48
00:02:33.317 --> 00:02:44.853
Students use their own knowledge. But you must decide only from the text. If the text says nothing, the answer is Not Given, even if the statement seems true in real life.

49
00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:48.135
How is False different from Not Given?

50
00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:57.429
False means the text contradicts the statement. Not Given means the text does not contain enough information to agree or disagree.

51
00:02:58.517 --> 00:03:00.075
Any warning words?

52
00:03:01.183 --> 00:03:14.067
Watch for words like all, only, never and always. A statement may exaggerate the text. If the text says most, but the statement says all, the answer is usually False.

53
00:03:15.233 --> 00:03:16.815
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:22.598
Grammar focus: Strategy: find evidence in the text, not in your head.

55
00:03:22.898 --> 00:03:24.167
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:26.722
If the text agrees, write True.

57
00:03:27.617 --> 00:03:31.009
If the text says nothing, write Not Given, not False.

58
00:03:31.967 --> 00:03:39.960
Locate the sentence, compare the meaning, and decide. Never use outside knowledge, and check for extreme words such as all, only and never.

59
00:03:40.717 --> 00:03:45.122
Be careful! Don't say: If the text says nothing, I write False.

60
00:03:47.067 --> 00:03:50.018
Say: If the text says nothing, I write Not Given.

61
00:03:50.318 --> 00:03:51.946
Nothing means Not Given.

62
00:03:52.733 --> 00:03:55.420
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:56.233 --> 00:03:57.757
A Report on Tea.

64
00:03:58.057 --> 00:04:01.554
The text says that most tea drinkers in the town prefer green tea.

65
00:04:02.567 --> 00:04:04.798
It does not mention the price of tea.

66
00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.833
A statement says all tea drinkers prefer green tea.

67
00:04:09.833 --> 00:04:12.483
Another statement says tea is cheap in the town.

68
00:04:13.467 --> 00:04:16.488
The first statement is False and the second is Not Given.

69
00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.741
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:22.583 --> 00:04:25.361
If the text says the opposite, the answer is blank.

71
00:04:25.661 --> 00:04:29.128
A, Not Given. B, False. C, True.

72
00:04:32.667 --> 00:04:34.724
The answer is B: False.

73
00:04:35.883 --> 00:04:38.591
If the text has no information, write blank.

74
00:04:38.891 --> 00:04:42.219
A, True. B, False. C, Not Given.

75
00:04:45.767 --> 00:04:47.720
The answer is C: Not Given.

76
00:04:48.883 --> 00:04:53.361
The text says most. The statement says all. The answer is usually blank.

77
00:04:53.661 --> 00:04:57.013
A, False. B, True. C, Not Given.

78
00:05:00.567 --> 00:05:02.787
The answer is A: False.

79
00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:06.263
Which word often shows an exaggeration?

80
00:05:06.563 --> 00:05:09.601
A, often. B, always. C, some.

81
00:05:13.167 --> 00:05:15.189
The answer is B: always.

82
00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:18.721
The price of tea is blank in the text.

83
00:05:19.021 --> 00:05:23.487
A, very high. B, very low. C, not mentioned.

84
00:05:27.033 --> 00:05:29.044
The answer is C: not mentioned.

85
00:05:30.233 --> 00:05:33.060
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:33.917 --> 00:05:36.485
How do you decide between False and Not Given?

87
00:05:45.283 --> 00:05:46.679
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:46.979 --> 00:06:01.293
I find the sentence in the text that matches the statement. If the text says the opposite, the answer is False. If the text does not give enough information, the answer is Not Given. I never use my own knowledge to answer.

89
00:06:01.593 --> 00:06:03.221
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:06:04.117 --> 00:06:10.449
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and strategy: find evidence in the text, not in your head.

91
00:06:10.749 --> 00:06:16.467
Today's value is intellectual honesty. Follow the evidence, even when it is inconvenient.

92
00:06:16.767 --> 00:06:19.233
See you in Unit eight. Goodbye!

93
00:06:19.533 --> 00:06:22.720
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

